-- title:
--   Surjectivity of the projection from the abstract Hecke algebra
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a nonzero natural number, used here purely as a level (no primality is assumed, despite the name of the variable). Write $\mathbb{T} =$ `HeckeAlg` for the polynomial ring $\mathbb{Z}[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ in one indeterminate for each prime, and let $\mathbb{T}(p) =$ [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra p 2 ∅`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) be the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}\bigl(\mathrm{CuspForm}(\Gamma_0(p), 2)\bigr)$ generated by the set [`CuspForm.heckeGenerators p 2 ∅`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L14). The map `heckeProj p : HeckeAlg →+* CuspForm.heckeAlgebra p 2 ∅` is the ring homomorphism underlying the $\mathbb{Z}$-algebra evaluation that sends the indeterminate indexed by a prime $\ell$ to `heckeProjGen p ℓ`, namely to the operator $U_\ell$ on weight-two cusp forms for $\Gamma_0(p)$ when $\ell \mid p$, and to $T_\ell$ when $\ell \nmid p$ (in both cases taken with the empty set of excluded primes, the required non-membership being automatic). The assertion is that this homomorphism is surjective as a function: every element of $\mathbb{T}(p)$ is the image of some polynomial in $\mathbb{T}$.
--
--   This is the standard presentation of the weight-two Hecke algebra of level $N$ as a quotient of an abstract polynomial Hecke algebra on the primes, in the form used by Mazur in his study of the Eisenstein ideal. It is the interface fact allowing statements about ideals and characters of $\mathbb{T}$ to be transported to $\mathbb{T}(p)$, and is cited in the construction of Eisenstein eigenvalue characters and in the identification of elements of the Eisenstein ideal.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_heckeProj_surjective.lean

